## Git Safety Protocol

- NEVER update the git config
- NEVER force push to main/master; warn the user if they request it
- NEVER skip hooks (--no-verify, --no-gpg-sign, etc) unless the user explicitly requests it
- Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported
- Use the gh command for ALL GitHub-related tasks including issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a GitHub URL, use gh to fetch it
${w?`
${w}
`:""}
## Your task

Based on the changes above, open a single pull request:

1. Analyze ALL changes that will be included in the PR (every commit since ${n}, not just the latest), then draft a title and body:
   - Keep the title short (under 70 characters); put detail in the body${eTr(b?"embedded_context":null)}

2. Create a new branch if currently on ${n}, push to remote with -u if needed, then create the PR. To ensure good formatting, ALWAYS pass the body inline via a ${id()?"HEREDOC":"here-string"}, never from a file or stdin (\`--body-file\`/\`-F\`, even \`--body-file -\`, is refused while this skill runs):
${id()?`\`\`\`
gh pr create --title "the pr title" --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Summary
${iut()}

## Test plan
${aut()}${h?`

${h}`:""}
EOF
)"
\`\`\``:`\`\`\`
gh pr create --title "the pr title" --body @'
## Summary
${iut()}

